in the ancient architecture, is a building where the columns stand very close to each other; only one diameter and a half of the column being allowed for the intercolumniations.

According to Mr Evelyn, the pycnotyle chiefly belonged to the composite order, and was used in the most magnificent buildings; as at present in the penityle at St Peter's at Rome, which consists of near 300 columns;
Pyrgus Iunus; and in such as yet remain of the ancients, among the ruins of Palmyra.
